(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-2-heart-great-vessels-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels — Anatomy Exam 2 — Set 2","category":"Anatomy","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is systole, and what is diastole?","choices":["Both terms describe atrial contraction only, with ventricular activity constant throughout the cardiac cycle","Systole is contraction of the ventricles (ejecting blood into the pulmonary trunk/aorta); diastole is relaxation of the ventricles (chambers filling with blood)","Systole is relaxation of the ventricles, during which blood is ejected into the pulmonary trunk and aorta","Systole and diastole both refer to the same phase of the cardiac cycle, occurring simultaneously in atria and ventricles"],"answer":1,"exp":"Systole is contraction of the ventricles, during which blood is ejected from the ventricles into the pulmonary trunk and the ascending aorta; diastole is relaxation of the ventricles, during which the chambers fill with blood. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"What is the sinoatrial (SA) node, and where is it located?","choices":["A valve structure in the right atrium that prevents backflow of blood into the superior vena cava","A relay station in the left ventricle that amplifies electrical signals before reaching the Purkinje fibers","A node found only within the AV bundle, embedded in the interventricular septum","The cardiac pacemaker, embedded in the superior posterior wall of the right atrium"],"answer":3,"exp":"The sinoatrial (SA) node is the cardiac pacemaker, embedded in the superior posterior wall of the right atrium; it determines the rate of heart contractions. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"What are bradycardia, normocardia, and tachycardia, in terms of heart rate?","choices":["Normocardia refers to a rate above 150 bpm, while bradycardia and tachycardia both describe rates below 60 bpm","Bradycardia: slow heart rate; normocardia: normal (60-100 bpm); tachycardia: rapid heart rate","Bradycardia: rapid heart rate; tachycardia: slow heart rate, with normocardia falling in between","All three terms describe the identical heart rate, differing only in the clinical context used"],"answer":1,"exp":"Bradycardia is a slow heart rate, normocardia is a normal heart rate (60-100 bpm), and tachycardia is a rapid heart rate. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"How does the impulse from the SA node travel to the AV node?","choices":["Via the coronary sinus","Via the vagus nerve","Via the internodal pathways","Directly through the ventricular myocardium, bypassing any pathway"],"answer":2,"exp":"The impulse travels from the SA node to the atrioventricular (AV) node via the internodal pathways. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Where is the AV node located, and how does its position compare to the SA node?","choices":["Sits in the superior posterior wall of the left atrium, identical position to the SA node","Sits in the left atrium, near the base of the mitral valve annulus","Sits within the floor or inferomedial aspect of the right atrium; it is more anterior than the SA node","Sits in the right ventricle; it is more inferior than the SA node"],"answer":2,"exp":"The AV node sits within the floor or inferomedial aspect of the right atrium; it is more anterior than the SA node. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What is the pathway of the impulse after leaving the AV node, through the bundle branches and Purkinje fibers?","choices":["AV node → AV bundle (travels along interventricular septum, divides into right and left bundle branches) → Purkinje fibers → ventricular myocardium","AV node → coronary sinus → ventricular myocardium, bypassing the bundle branches and Purkinje fibers entirely","AV bundle → AV node → Purkinje fibers → SA node, forming a closed conduction loop that circles back to the primary pacemaker","AV node → Purkinje fibers directly, bypassing the AV bundle and bundle branches, reaching the ventricles instantly"],"answer":0,"exp":"The impulse travels from the AV node to the AV bundle, which travels along the interventricular septum and then divides to form the right and left bundle branches; the bundle branches send impulses to the Purkinje fibers, which convey impulses to the ventricular myocardium. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What neurotransmitter mediates parasympathetic stimulation of the heart, and what is its overall effect?","choices":["Norepinephrine; increases heart rate through beta-1 adrenergic receptor stimulation","Dopamine; only affects blood pressure, not heart rate directly","Epinephrine; has no effect on heart rate, acting solely on vascular smooth muscle","Acetylcholine; opposes the sympathetic nervous system and slows heart rate via vagal tone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Parasympathetic nervous system stimulation of the heart is mediated by acetylcholine, opposing the sympathetic nervous system; parasympathetic input dominates the autonomic stimulation at rest, slowing heart rate via vagal tone, and the vagus nerve controlling this arises from the vagal nuclei in the brainstem. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Besides controlling heart rate, what other sensory function does the vagus nerve serve for the heart?","choices":["Produces atrial natriuretic peptide","Has no sensory function related to the heart","Senses pain for the heart","Regulates coronary blood flow directly via smooth muscle contraction"],"answer":2,"exp":"The vagus nerve also senses pain for the heart, in addition to its parasympathetic motor role slowing heart rate. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"What effect does sympathetic innervation have on the heart, and from which spinal cord segments does it arise?","choices":["Increases the rate and force of heart contractions; arises from T1-T5 spinal cord segments","Decreases the rate and force of contractions; arises from T1-T5 spinal cord segments via the vagus nerve","Has no effect on heart rate, only force; arises from lumbar segments","Increases contraction force only; arises from the sacral spinal cord segments"],"answer":0,"exp":"Sympathetic innervation increases both the rate and the force of heart contractions, arising from the T1-T5 segments of the spinal cord; preganglionic fibers enter the sympathetic trunk and ascend through ganglia, and postganglionic fibers travel through the heart via cardiac nerves. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What do coronary blood vessels supply, and where do the major coronary arteries branch from?","choices":["Supply blood to the lungs; branch off the pulmonary trunk near the pulmonary valve","Supply blood to the brain; branch off the aortic arch's carotid branches","Supply blood to the kidneys; branch off the abdominal aorta below the renal hilum","Supply blood to the heart muscles; branch off the ascending aorta near its base"],"answer":3,"exp":"Coronary blood vessels supply blood to the heart muscles, with the major coronary vessels (right and left coronary arteries) branching off the ascending aorta near its base. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Where does the right coronary artery (RCA) pass, and what are its major branches?","choices":["Passes through the interventricular septum exclusively, giving rise only to small septal perforating branches that feed the conduction system","Has no described branches at all, running as a single continuous, unbranching vessel around the right atrioventricular groove toward the apex","Passes between the right auricle and right ventricle; major branches include atrial branches, right marginal branch, posterior interventricular branch, and conducting system branches","Passes between the left atrium and left ventricle; branches only to the left ventricle's posterior wall via its circumflex continuation near the groove"],"answer":2,"exp":"The right coronary artery (RCA) passes between the right auricle and right ventricle; its major branches include atrial branches, the right marginal branch, the posterior interventricular branch, and conducting system branches. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"In what percentage of individuals does the RCA supply the SA node, and what does the RCA supply in general regarding the AV node?","choices":["RCA supplies the SA node in 100% of patients, with no anatomical variation described in the population","RCA supplies the AV node only 10% of the time, with the LCA supplying it in the remaining 90%","RCA never supplies the SA node; only the LCA does, via its circumflex branch in all patients","RCA supplies the SA node in 60% of patients (LCA via circumflex branch in 40%); RCA supplies the AV node"],"answer":3,"exp":"The RCA supplies the SA node in 60% of patients (with the LCA supplying it via the circumflex branch in the remaining 40%); the RCA supplies the AV node. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"In what percentage of individuals does the posterior interventricular branch arise from the RCA versus the LCA?","choices":["50/50 split with no variation","67-85% from the RCA; 15-33% from the LCA","67-85% from the LCA; 15-33% from the RCA","100% from the RCA, with no LCA contribution"],"answer":1,"exp":"The posterior interventricular branch arises from the RCA in 67-85% of individuals, and from the LCA in 15-33% of individuals. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What are the major branches of the left coronary artery (LCA)?","choices":["Posterior interventricular branch only, arising directly from the main trunk of the left coronary artery","Atrial branches only, with no ventricular branches, supplying solely the left atrial wall","Only the right marginal branch, extending from the LCA toward the right atrioventricular groove","Circumflex branch (forming the left marginal branch and posterior left ventricular branch) and anterior interventricular branch"],"answer":3,"exp":"The major branches of the left coronary artery are the circumflex branch (which branches to form the left marginal branch and the posterior left ventricular branch) and the anterior interventricular branch (which connects to the posterior interventricular branch via anastomoses). — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What supplies the interventricular septum, and what supplies the anterolateral versus posteromedial papillary muscles?","choices":["Neither papillary muscle receives coronary blood supply, relying instead on diffusion from ventricular blood","Interventricular septum is mostly LCA; anterolateral papillary muscle is supplied by LCA branches, posteromedial by the RCA's posterior interventricular branch","Interventricular septum is mostly RCA; both papillary muscles are supplied by the LCA's circumflex branch","The interventricular septum receives no coronary blood supply at all, relying solely on endocardial diffusion"],"answer":1,"exp":"The interventricular septum is mostly supplied by the LCA; the anterolateral papillary muscle is supplied by the left anterior interventricular branch and circumflex branch (both LCA), while the posteromedial papillary muscle is supplied by the posterior interventricular branch (RCA). — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What are the named cardiac veins, and where do they all eventually drain?","choices":["All cardiac veins drain into the left atrium, delivering deoxygenated blood directly into systemic circulation rather than returning it to the right heart","Great cardiac vein, middle cardiac vein, posterior vein of the left ventricle, small cardiac vein, and anterior cardiac veins; all eventually lead to the coronary sinus, which drains into the right atrium","Only one cardiac vein exists: the great cardiac vein, which drains directly into the inferior vena cava without passing through the coronary sinus at all","All cardiac veins drain directly into the aorta, mixing deoxygenated blood with oxygenated systemic blood before it reaches the peripheral tissues"],"answer":1,"exp":"The cardiac veins include the great cardiac vein, middle cardiac vein, posterior vein of the left ventricle, small cardiac vein, and anterior cardiac veins; all of these eventually lead to the coronary sinus, which drains into the right atrium. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Where does the ascending aorta begin, and what are its only branches?","choices":["Begins at the aortic arch; branches include the carotid arteries supplying the head and neck","Begins at the aortic valve; the left and right coronary arteries are its only branches","Begins at the AV node; branches to the ventricles via the interventricular septum","Begins at the pulmonary valve; has no branches until it reaches the aortic arch"],"answer":1,"exp":"The ascending aorta begins at the aortic valve; the left and right coronary arteries, which branch off its base, are the only branches of the ascending aorta. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"What three vessels does the aortic arch branch into?","choices":["Right and left coronary arteries only, supplying the myocardium before the arch continues distally","Superior and inferior vena cava, draining directly into the arch's concave inner curvature","Brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid artery, and left subclavian artery","Pulmonary trunk and pulmonary arteries, carrying deoxygenated blood toward the lungs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Blood in the aortic arch branches into the brachiocephalic trunk, the left common carotid artery, and the left subclavian artery (with the aortic arch continuing on as the descending aorta). — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What does the brachiocephalic trunk give rise to?","choices":["The right common carotid artery and the right subclavian artery","The celiac trunk and superior mesenteric artery, supplying the upper abdominal organs","The left subclavian artery only, supplying the left upper extremity","The left common carotid artery only, supplying the left side of the head"],"answer":0,"exp":"The brachiocephalic trunk gives rise to the right common carotid artery (supplying the right side of the head/brain) and the right subclavian artery (supplying the right arm). — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"What does the left common carotid artery supply, and what does it divide into?","choices":["Supplies the left leg; divides into the femoral and popliteal arteries below the inguinal ligament","Supplies the pelvic viscera; divides into the internal and external iliac arteries","Supplies the right arm; divides into the radial and ulnar arteries at the elbow","Supplies the left side of the head and brain; divides into internal and external carotid arteries"],"answer":3,"exp":"The left common carotid artery supplies blood to the left side of the head and brain, dividing into the internal and external carotid arteries (the internal carotid has no branches until it's in the cranium, while the external carotid begins branching and feeds the neck and face). — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"Where does the descending aorta divide into thoracic and abdominal aorta, and at what level?","choices":["At the level of the aortic valve","It never divides; \"thoracic\" and \"abdominal\" describe the same continuous vessel with no anatomical distinction","At the level of the renal arteries only","As it pierces the aortic hiatus of the diaphragm, at the T12 level"],"answer":3,"exp":"The descending aorta, a continuation of the aortic arch, is divided into the thoracic aorta and abdominal aorta as the artery pierces the aortic hiatus of the diaphragm at the T12 level. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"What vessels does the thoracic aorta branch to form?","choices":["Renal, suprarenal, and gonadal arteries, supplying the kidneys, adrenal glands, and reproductive organs","Bronchial, pericardial, mediastinal, esophageal, intercostal, and superior phrenic arteries","Common iliac and internal iliac arteries, supplying the pelvis and lower extremities","Renal artery, superior mesenteric artery, and inferior phrenic artery"],"answer":1,"exp":"The thoracic aorta branches to form the bronchial, pericardial, mediastinal, esophageal, intercostal, and superior phrenic arteries. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What major vessels does the abdominal aorta branch to form?","choices":["Only the coronary arteries, which branch from the abdominal aorta near the diaphragm to supply the myocardium","Celiac trunk, superior mesenteric artery, inferior mesenteric artery, inferior phrenic/suprarenal/renal/gonadal/lumbar arteries, and common iliac arteries","Only the carotid and subclavian arteries, arising from the abdominal aorta to supply the head and upper limbs","Bronchial, pericardial, and mediastinal arteries, supplying the lungs, heart covering, and chest structures"],"answer":1,"exp":"The abdominal aorta branches to form the celiac trunk, superior mesenteric artery, inferior mesenteric artery, inferior phrenic arteries, suprarenal arteries, renal arteries, gonadal arteries, lumbar arteries, and the right and left common iliac arteries. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"What organs does the celiac trunk supply?","choices":["Kidneys and adrenal glands only, via the renal and suprarenal branches","Liver, stomach, esophagus, gallbladder, duodenum, pancreas, and spleen","The lower limbs only, via the femoral and popliteal arteries","The heart and lungs, via the coronary and pulmonary circuits"],"answer":1,"exp":"The celiac trunk supplies the liver, stomach, esophagus, gallbladder, duodenum, pancreas, and spleen. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"What does the splenic artery (a celiac trunk branch) supply, and what does the left gastric artery supply?","choices":["Both arteries supply the pancreas exclusively, via small branching pancreatic vessels","Splenic artery supplies the pancreas tail; left gastric artery supplies the esophagus","Splenic artery supplies the spleen; left gastric artery supplies the lesser curvature of the stomach","Splenic artery supplies the kidney; left gastric artery supplies the duodenum"],"answer":2,"exp":"The splenic artery, a celiac trunk branch, supplies the spleen; the left gastric artery supplies the lesser curvature of the stomach. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"What does the common hepatic artery branch to form, and what does the cystic artery supply?","choices":["Branches to form the superior mesenteric artery; the cystic artery supplies the spleen's lower pole","Branches to form the hepatic artery proper, right gastric artery, and gastroduodenal artery; the cystic artery supplies the gallbladder","Branches to form only the left gastric artery; the cystic artery supplies the duodenum","Has no further branches; the cystic artery supplies the stomach's lesser curvature"],"answer":1,"exp":"The common hepatic artery branches to form the hepatic artery proper (supplies the liver), the right gastric artery (supplies the lesser curvature of the stomach), and the gastroduodenal artery (supplies the duodenum and inferior greater curvature of the stomach); the cystic artery, a further branch, supplies the gallbladder. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"What does the superior mesenteric artery supply, versus the inferior mesenteric artery?","choices":["Superior mesenteric supplies only the rectum via its terminal branches, while the inferior mesenteric supplies only the stomach via ascending branches","Neither artery supplies any abdominal organ directly, since both function solely as anastomotic connections linking the celiac trunk to the iliac system","Both arteries supply identical territory, running in parallel along the mesentery to double the blood supply reaching the small and large intestine equally","Superior mesenteric: pancreas/duodenum/small intestine/large intestine (via right colic, middle colic, ileocolic arteries); Inferior mesenteric: terminal large intestine/rectum (via left colic, sigmoid, superior rectal arteries)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The superior mesenteric artery supplies the pancreas, duodenum, small intestine, and part of the large intestine (via the right colic, middle colic, and ileocolic arteries), while the inferior mesenteric artery supplies the terminal portion of the large intestine and rectum (via the left colic, sigmoid, and superior rectal arteries). — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"What do the internal iliac artery and external iliac artery each supply?","choices":["Both arteries supply identical territory, splitting equally between the pelvic organs and lower extremities","Neither artery is a branch of the common iliac artery, arising instead independently from the descending aorta","Internal iliac: urinary bladder, pelvic walls, external genitalia, medial thigh; External iliac: continues to become the femoral artery, supplying the lower extremities","Internal iliac supplies the lower extremities directly; external iliac remains confined to the pelvis, supplying the bladder, genitalia, and pelvic floor"],"answer":2,"exp":"The internal iliac artery supplies the urinary bladder, walls of the pelvis, external genitalia, and medial side of the thigh, remaining in the pelvis; the external iliac artery continues and becomes the femoral artery, supplying the lower extremities. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"What are the five paired (left and right) arteries that branch off the descending aorta?","choices":["Inferior phrenic, suprarenal, renal, gonadal, and lumbar arteries","Coronary arteries and pulmonary arteries","Common carotid and subclavian arteries","Celiac trunk, superior mesenteric, and inferior mesenteric arteries (unpaired)"],"answer":0,"exp":"Five paired (left and right) arteries branch off the descending aorta: the inferior phrenic arteries, suprarenal arteries, renal arteries, gonadal arteries, and lumbar arteries. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"Why are the gonadal arteries sometimes called testicular or ovarian arteries instead?","choices":["The terms testicular/ovarian describe a different artery pair entirely, unrelated to the gonadal arteries","The name changes depending on sex once the vessel can be seen supplying the visible reproductive organs","The names are used interchangeably at random, with no anatomical basis","Only male anatomy has gonadal arteries; \"ovarian artery\" is an entirely separate, unrelated vessel"],"answer":1,"exp":"The gonadal arteries are called testicular arteries or ovarian arteries depending on sex, once the vessel can be seen supplying the visible reproductive organs (they are simply no longer called \"gonadal arteries\" at that point). — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 97"}]};
